Monthly assumptions audit of the ClaudeOS workspace at ${BORG_ROOT}.

This audit re-evaluates ephemeral best-practice assumptions — the thresholds and
tooling choices that shift as Claude Code, models, and the harness evolve. Some
are codified as lint rules (e.g. the AGENTS.md size ceiling), some aren't. The
lint audit enforces conformance to whatever value is written; this audit asks
whether that written value or choice is still right.

When an assumption is flagged RECONSIDER or UPDATE, the report must name **where
the fix lands** — the canonical file(s) that hold the codified value or choice —
so the change can be made without re-deriving it. Each assumption below ends
with a "Where to update if flagged" pointer; carry it into the report.

STEP 1: This job is fired by launchd on calendar days 1-5 of each month, but
should only execute once per month. Stop and output nothing if:
- ${BORG_ROOT}/c4po/.claude/scheduled/state/c4po-assumptions-audit-monthly.json
  exists and its `date` field is in the current calendar month (already ran
  this month).

The scheduler retries on subsequent days if an earlier attempt was missed
(e.g., laptop asleep).

STEP 2: Re-evaluate each assumption below. For each, decide one of:
- STILL VALID — no change, nothing to report.
- RECONSIDER — likely outdated; explain why in one sentence.
- UPDATE — propose a specific replacement in one sentence.

Be honest. These are intentionally ephemeral; "STILL VALID" should be earned,
not defaulted to.

Assumption A — canonical instruction-file size ceiling
  Canonical home: the ceiling is codified as a lint rule in
  ${BORG_ROOT}/LINT.md → Size. The lint audit enforces conformance to
  that number (and flags over-limit files); this audit re-evaluates whether the
  number itself is still right and whether content should move out of AGENTS.md.
  Do NOT re-scan for over-limit files here — that is the lint audit's job.
  Re-evaluate in three steps:
  1. Search the GENERAL web for current sizing recommendations for both Codex
     `AGENTS.md` and Claude Code `CLAUDE.md`, since the same canonical content is
     loaded by both tools. Include the current year and weigh official guidance,
     developer blogs, forums, GitHub, and practitioner reports.
  2. Balance community practice against the latest official OpenAI Codex and
     Anthropic Claude Code recommendations. Where they diverge, choose a ceiling
     safe for both tools and note the gap in one line.
  3. Read every AGENTS.md under ${BORG_ROOT} (exclude .git, node_modules, dist,
     build, and any *_bak_* backup files) and look for content that should be
     moved OUT of AGENTS.md into a skill or a scoped rule — durable procedures,
     reusable multi-step workflows, or domain knowledge that doesn't need to
     load every session. AGENTS.md should hold only always-on context.
  Flag UPDATE if steps 1–2 show the codified ceiling is outdated (give the new
  number) and/or step 3 finds relocatable content (name the file and what
  should move where).
  Where to update if flagged: the **Size** rule in ${BORG_ROOT}/LINT.md for
  the ceiling number; the specific AGENTS.md file(s) named for content moves.

Assumption B — launchd is the right scheduler
  Current choice: macOS launchd, with model prompts driven by
  ${BORG_ROOT}/.bin/run-scheduled-task.sh and deterministic model-less
  maintenance jobs using dedicated shell runners.
  Consider alternatives that may have matured: Claude Code's native scheduled
  tasks (the `schedule` skill, `/loop`, scheduled-tasks MCP, routine plugins),
  or other cron-like tools. Has any alternative become a clearly better fit
  for this workspace? Weigh reliability, observability, ease of edit,
  portability across machines, and ongoing maintenance burden. Use WebSearch
  if helpful to check what's current.
  Where to update if flagged: the scheduler implementation —
  ${BORG_ROOT}/.bin/run-scheduled-task.sh and the `com.theborg.*` launchd
  plists — plus the scheduled-tasks documentation in ${BORG_ROOT}/README.md.

Assumption C — Slash commands remain useful and unsuperseded
  Enumerate every live `*/.claude/commands/*.md` under ${BORG_ROOT}, excluding
  the inert bernard case study and generated/vendored directories. These files
  are canonical for Claude Code; ${BORG_ROOT}/.bin/sync-codex-prompts.sh adapts
  them into Codex skills invoked as `$name` or selected through `/skills`.
  For each command, decide whether it still earns its place or has been
  superseded by a built-in skill, plugin, or harness feature. Flag any
  individual command as RECONSIDER or UPDATE rather than the whole assumption,
  if some are fine and others aren't.
  Also check whether Codex has replaced or changed its skill authoring or
  explicit-invocation surface; if so, flag the bridge for UPDATE. Do not
  recommend another surface unless it preserves the workflows' explicit
  invocation semantics or that behavior change is justified.
  Where to update if flagged: the specific command file under
  `.claude/commands/`; if a command is retired, also its paired `.prompt` (for
  scheduled commands) and the slash-command listing in ${BORG_ROOT}/README.md.

Assumption D — cerebruh raw source ceiling (75k tokens per sub-wiki)
  Current guideline (${BORG_ROOT}/cerebruh/AGENTS.md): each
  `cerebruh/wikis/<sub-wiki>/raw/` directory should hold less than about 75,000
  tokens of raw source documents; exceeding it triggers a proposed split.
  This ceiling is ephemeral — it tracks context-window size, model capability,
  and the retrieval pattern (full-context read vs. RAG). Use WebSearch to check
  whether the rationale behind it still holds (search terms like "LLM wiki
  context window scaling limit" or "Obsidian knowledge base RAG threshold"); if
  a different ceiling or a retrieval change makes the 75k number outdated, flag
  UPDATE with the new guidance.
  Then estimate the total raw-source token count of each sub-wiki: for every
  `cerebruh/wikis/<sub-wiki>/raw/` directory, sum its source files and
  approximate tokens as bytes/4 — but **only for text-native sources** (.md,
  .txt, .html, .json). For PDFs, raw file size is not a token proxy: embedded
  page images and fonts dominate the bytes and inflate the estimate several-fold
  (measured 2.3x on this corpus in the 2026-08 audit). Extract the PDF's text
  layer and use extracted-bytes/4 instead. If a PDF has no text layer (a pure
  scan), say so and treat its token count as unknown rather than guessing from
  file size. List any sub-wiki whose estimate exceeds the current ceiling along
  with its estimated token count and which method produced it, so an over-limit
  sub-wiki can be proposed for a split.
  Where to update if flagged: the 75k-token ceiling lives in
  ${BORG_ROOT}/cerebruh/AGENTS.md (editable as lint scaffolding); over-limit
  sub-wikis are split through cerebruh's own ingest workflow, not here.

Assumption E — "dreaming" is still a custom job, not a native feature
  Current choice: memory maintenance is implemented as one custom c4po job and
  the multi-session scope of a workspace slash command — `c4po-retro` /
  `/retro sessions` (harvest durable lessons from sessions into proposed rules,
  AGENTS.md edits, skills, and cerebruh ingest candidates). Renamed from
  `c4po-dream` / `/dream` on 2026-08-01 to free the name ahead of Anthropic's
  native `/dream`, and folded into the existing single-session `/retro` rather
  than keeping a second command. (A companion `c4po-consolidate-memory` job was
  decommissioned 2026-07-14 after its underlying built-in skill disappeared from
  the CLI.)
  The job exists because Claude Code and Claude Desktop have no
  built-in "dreaming" / memory-maintenance feature today; the only first-party
  "dreams" capability lives in Claude Managed Agents, which this setup doesn't use.
  This assumption is ephemeral by design — Anthropic is expected to add native
  dreaming to Claude Code or Claude Desktop eventually. Use WebSearch (anthropic.com,
  docs.claude.com, code.claude.com, plus the general web/forums/GitHub; search terms
  like "Claude Code memory dream", "Claude Desktop memory consolidation",
  "Claude Code automatic memory maintenance", plus the current year) to check
  whether a native feature now covers what this job does. Flag:
  - RECONSIDER if a partial/preview native capability has appeared that overlaps
    the job but doesn't fully replace it (note what it covers).
  - UPDATE if a native feature now does the harvesting, so the custom job and
    command can be retired or migrated onto it.
  Where to update if flagged: the prompt
  ${BORG_ROOT}/c4po/.claude/scheduled/c4po-retro.prompt; the Multi-session scope
  section of ${BORG_ROOT}/.claude/commands/retro.md (retiring the job means
  removing that section and its dispatch, not the whole command — the
  single-session retro stands on its own); the TASKS rows in
  ${BORG_ROOT}/.bin/install-scheduled-tasks.sh and the `com.theborg.*` plists; and
  the scheduled-jobs + slash-command listings in ${BORG_ROOT}/README.md.

Assumption F — default Claude model set in the scheduled-task runner
  Current choice: every headless `claude -p` scheduled run uses the `opus` alias
  via the `MODEL` variable in the runner
  (${BORG_ROOT}/.bin/run-scheduled-task.sh), which passes it as `--model`. The
  setting lives in the runner — NOT the user-level `model` field in
  `~/.claude/settings.json` — deliberately: that settings field is mutated by
  any interactive `/model` toggle, and a drift there onto a credits-gated model
  (Fable 5) hard-failed the security audit 2026-07-21. The alias pins the family,
  not a version: it resolves to the latest GA Opus, so a new Opus generation is
  adopted without an edit. Repo-hosted tasks under `repos/*` may override the
  model for their own run via the `MODEL` key in their `.conf` sidecar (sourced
  by the runner) — that lives in the repo, not here, and is out of scope.
  Verify two things:
  1. The setting is intact: `${BORG_ROOT}/.bin/run-scheduled-task.sh` still sets
     `MODEL=opus` as the default and passes `--model "$MODEL"` on the `claude -p`
     invocations. Flag RECONSIDER if it's missing, no longer passed, or holds a
     different default (something changed it outside this audit).
  2. Opus is still the right default family: use WebSearch (anthropic.com,
     docs.claude.com, code.claude.com) to check whether the alias still resolves
     to a model appropriate for these jobs, and whether Anthropic has shipped a
     GA tier that should displace Opus as the workspace default. Flag UPDATE with
     the replacement alias if so.
  Where to update if flagged: the `MODEL` default in
  ${BORG_ROOT}/.bin/run-scheduled-task.sh, and this assumption's pinned value so
  the next audit verifies the new choice.

Assumption G — the LLM Wiki pattern is the best lightweight RAG for this setup
  Current choice: cerebruh implements Andrej Karpathy's "LLM Wiki" pattern
  (${BORG_ROOT}/cerebruh/AGENTS.md) — Claude-maintained interlinked markdown
  pages retrieved by reading `wikis/index.md`, then a sub-wiki's `wiki/index.md`,
  then the relevant pages into full context. There is no embedding/vector index;
  retrieval is index-guided full-context read. This suits small, curated
  corpora, but the right approach is ephemeral — it tracks corpus size, context
  window, and the maturity of lightweight retrieval tooling.
  Re-evaluate in two steps:
  1. Estimate the workspace's data volume, measuring what retrieval actually
     loads. Raw sources are never read by the Question-Answering workflow, so
     size the wiki side first and treat raw as background:
     - Retrieval corpus: sum `cerebruh/wikis/<sub-wiki>/wiki/**.md` at bytes/4
       (markdown, so bytes/4 is sound), and note the page count and mean page
       size.
     - Per-query fixed cost: the size of `wikis/index.md` plus the **largest**
       sub-wiki `wiki/index.md`. Every query in that domain pays this before it
       reads a single page, so it is the pattern's real pressure point — call it
       out with the sub-wiki name.
     - Background: total raw-source tokens across all `raw/` directories, reusing
       Assumption D's per-format estimates (text-native bytes/4, PDFs via text
       extraction).
  2. Use WebSearch to check whether a lightweight RAG approach has become a
     clearly better fit at this data volume (search terms like "lightweight RAG
     vs full context 2026", "LLM wiki pattern vs vector search", "Claude Code
     semantic search / retrieval MCP", "agentic retrieval small corpus"). Weigh
     retrieval quality, setup/maintenance burden, portability, and whether the
     corpus has outgrown index-guided full-context read.
  Flag:
  - STILL VALID if the LLM Wiki pattern remains the best fit at the current
    data volume (small enough that full-context read beats the complexity of a
    vector/embedding pipeline).
  - RECONSIDER if the corpus is approaching a size where the pattern strains
    but no alternative is a clear win yet (note the pressure point).
  - UPDATE if a specific lightweight RAG approach is now clearly better for this
    data volume (name it and what would change).
  Where to update if flagged: the retrieval approach is codified in the
  Question-Answering workflow and folder structure of
  ${BORG_ROOT}/cerebruh/AGENTS.md (editable as lint scaffolding); the routing
  policy in ${BORG_ROOT}/AGENTS.md → "How to use the cerebruh knowledge base"
  may also need updating. Note that any structural change to cerebruh itself is
  made through cerebruh's own workflow, not here.

Assumption H — the rolling session-limit window is 5 hours
  The only value pinned here is the **5-hour** rolling session window — usage is
  capped per window starting from the window's first message. That is a vendor
  fact and the actual subject of this assumption. It must hold for whichever
  vendor drives the backlog burndown (today `codex exec` against a ChatGPT plan;
  the 5-hour rolling limit applies to both Anthropic and OpenAI plans).

  **Do not pin schedule ids, fire times, or reset times in this file.** They live
  in the scheduler and drift out of sync the moment a job is rescheduled. Read
  them at run time instead:
  1. Schedule id + fire times — from ${BORG_ROOT}/.bin/install-scheduled-tasks.sh:
     find the TASKS row whose task name is `c4po-backlog-burndown` to get its
     schedule id, then read that id's `cal_entry` lines in the schedule case
     block to get the two fire times. This is the source of truth.
  2. Weekly usage reset — from the WINDOW phase of
     ${BORG_ROOT}/c4po/.claude/scheduled/c4po-backlog-burndown.prompt. The reset
     is an account fact (read from the vendor's `/status`), not a scheduler fact,
     so it is legitimately recorded there.
  If either read fails (task row absent, schedule id has no case branch, WINDOW
  phase no longer states a reset), flag RECONSIDER and say which read failed —
  a broken read means the burndown's timing can no longer be verified at all.

  Check, in order:
  1. Use WebSearch (anthropic.com, docs.claude.com, support.claude.com, and the
     OpenAI/ChatGPT plan docs, plus community reports; search terms like "Claude
     usage limits 5-hour session window" and "ChatGPT Codex usage limit rolling
     window", plus the current year) to confirm the window is still 5 hours.
  2. Verify the fire times you read satisfy the invariant under the confirmed
     window length:
       second fire = reset − window + 10min slack
       first fire  = second fire − window − 10min
     The twin firing exists because one run's burn is capped by the session
     limit: the second must start just past the first's session-limit boundary
     and still land before the reset.
  Flag UPDATE if the window length changed (give the new length and the
  recomputed fire times) OR if the scheduled fire times no longer satisfy the
  invariant (give both the scheduled and the correct times). A drifted second
  run either lands still-limited (window grew) or wastes burnable time (window
  shrank).
  Where to update if flagged: the burndown's schedule id in
  ${BORG_ROOT}/.bin/install-scheduled-tasks.sh — quote the id you actually read,
  do not assume a previous run's — then re-run it with `--load`; the WINDOW phase
  of ${BORG_ROOT}/c4po/.claude/scheduled/c4po-backlog-burndown.prompt; and the
  burndown row in ${BORG_ROOT}/README.md. Only a changed **window length** is
  edited back into this file.

STEP 3: Always pipe a report into
`${BORG_ROOT}/.bin/notify-email.sh c4po "Borg assumptions audit — {YYYY-MM-DD} — {N} flagged"`
(reads message body from stdin) — even when every assumption is STILL VALID
(subject reads "— 0 flagged"), so the user can confirm the job ran.
Format:

  Borg assumptions audit - {YYYY-MM-DD}

Each non-STILL-VALID block ends with a "Where to update" line naming the
canonical file(s) to edit (from that assumption's "Where to update if flagged"
pointer above).

  Assumption A (size ceiling): {STILL VALID|RECONSIDER|UPDATE}
    {one-line reason if not STILL VALID — new number and/or what should move}
    Content to relocate:
      {path}: {what should move → which skill or rule}
      ...
    (omit the "Content to relocate" block if none)
    Where to update: {e.g. Size rule in ${BORG_ROOT}/LINT.md; <file(s)> for moves}

  Assumption B (launchd): {STILL VALID|RECONSIDER|UPDATE}
    {one-line reason if not STILL VALID}
    Where to update: {.bin/run-scheduled-task.sh, launchd plists, README.md}

  Assumption C (slash commands): {STILL VALID|RECONSIDER|UPDATE}
    {one-line reason if not STILL VALID}
    Per-command notes:
      {/command}: {verdict} - {one-line reason}
      ...
    (omit per-command block if all STILL VALID)
    Where to update: {the specific command file(s); paired .prompt + README.md if retired}

  Assumption D (raw source ceiling): {STILL VALID|RECONSIDER|UPDATE}
    {one-line reason if not STILL VALID}
    Over-limit sub-wikis:
      {sub-wiki}: ~{token_estimate} tokens ({bytes/4|PDF text extraction|mixed})
      ...
    (omit the "Over-limit sub-wikis" block if none)
    Where to update: {75k ceiling in ${BORG_ROOT}/cerebruh/AGENTS.md}

  Assumption E (native dreaming): {STILL VALID|RECONSIDER|UPDATE}
    {one-line reason if not STILL VALID — name the native feature that
     supersedes c4po-retro/`/retro sessions`}
    Where to update: {the superseded .prompt(s) + the Multi-session scope section of .claude/commands/retro.md; install-scheduled-tasks.sh TASKS rows + plists; README.md}

  Assumption F (default model): {STILL VALID|RECONSIDER|UPDATE}
    {one-line reason if not STILL VALID — the value actually found in
     .bin/run-scheduled-task.sh and/or the replacement model id}
    Where to update: {`MODEL` default in ${BORG_ROOT}/.bin/run-scheduled-task.sh; this assumption's pinned value in the .prompt}

  Assumption G (LLM Wiki RAG fit): {STILL VALID|RECONSIDER|UPDATE}
    Corpus: {P} pages / ~{T} tokens; per-query fixed cost ~{F} tokens
    (largest index: {sub-wiki})
    {one-line reason if not STILL VALID — the pressure point, and the
     alternative retrieval approach, if any, that now fits better}
    Where to update: {cerebruh/AGENTS.md retrieval workflow; cerebruh routing in ${BORG_ROOT}/AGENTS.md — changes to cerebruh made via its own workflow}

  Assumption H (session-limit window): {STILL VALID|RECONSIDER|UPDATE}
    Schedule read: {schedule id} fires {time 1} / {time 2}; reset {time}; window
    {N}h -> invariant {holds|violated}
    {one-line reason if not STILL VALID — the new window length and/or the
     recomputed fire times}
    Where to update: {the schedule id you read, in .bin/install-scheduled-tasks.sh (+ --load); c4po-backlog-burndown.prompt WINDOW; README.md; window length in this .prompt}

Omit any assumption block that is STILL VALID with nothing to add. If every
assumption is STILL VALID and every sub-wiki is within the raw source ceiling,
the body is a one-liner: `All assumptions STILL VALID — nothing to update.`
(AGENTS.md over-limit files are the lint audit's responsibility, not this
audit's.)

STEP 4: Write
${BORG_ROOT}/c4po/.claude/scheduled/state/c4po-assumptions-audit-monthly.json
with `{"date": "YYYY-MM-DD", "flagged": <count>}` so the run isn't repeated
this month. `mkdir -p` the state directory first if it doesn't exist. `flagged`
is the count of items (assumptions + sub-items) that were RECONSIDER or UPDATE;
0 means a clean audit.
